                        SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION TO QUEUE
===============================================================================

🔹 WHAT IS A QUEUE?
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

A Queue is a LINEAR DATA STRUCTURE that follows a particular order in which
operations are performed. The order is FIFO (First In First Out) or LILO 
(Last In Last Out).

Think of it like:
🎫 A ticket counter line - First person in line gets served first
🚶 Queue at a bus stop - People board in the order they arrived
🍔 McDonald's drive-thru - Orders are processed in sequence
📞 Call center - Calls are answered in the order received

🔹 KEY CHARACTERISTICS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✅ FIFO Principle: First element added is the first one removed
✅ Two Access Points: FRONT (removal) and REAR (insertion)
✅ Linear Flow: Elements move from REAR to FRONT
✅ Fast Operations: All basic operations are O(1) time complexity

🔹 REAL-WORLD APPLICATIONS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. CPU Scheduling
   - Process scheduling in operating systems
   - Round-robin scheduling algorithm

2. Printer Queue
   - Print jobs are processed in FIFO order
   - First document sent gets printed first

3. BFS (Breadth-First Search)
   - Level-order traversal of trees
   - Shortest path algorithms

4. Asynchronous Data Transfer
   - IO Buffers
   - Pipes, file IO operations

5. Customer Service
   - Call centers handling calls
   - Help desk ticketing systems

6. Network Packet Management
   - Routers managing packet transmission
   - Network traffic management

7. Web Server Request Handling
   - Handling incoming HTTP requests
   - Task queues in web applications

===============================================================================
                  SECTION 2: QUEUE OPERATIONS & TERMINOLOGY
===============================================================================

🔹 BASIC QUEUE OPERATIONS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. ENQUEUE (Insert/Add)
   ├─ Adds an element to the REAR of the queue
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Can cause Queue Overflow if queue is full

2. DEQUEUE (Remove/Delete)
   ├─ Removes and returns the FRONT element
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Can cause Queue Underflow if queue is empty

3. FRONT/PEEK (Access)
   ├─ Returns the FRONT element WITHOUT removing it
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Useful for checking what's at front

4. REAR (Access)
   ├─ Returns the REAR element WITHOUT removing it
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Useful for checking what was last added

5. isEmpty()
   ├─ Checks if queue is empty
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Returns true if no elements, false otherwise

6. isFull()
   ├─ Checks if queue is full (for array implementation)
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Only relevant for fixed-size arrays

7. size()
   ├─ Returns number of elements in queue
   ├─ Time Complexity: O(1)
   └─ Useful for knowing current queue size

🔹 QUEUE TERMINOLOGY:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

• FRONT: Pointer to the first element (for removal)
• REAR: Pointer to the last element (for insertion)
• CAPACITY: Maximum number of elements queue can hold
• SIZE: Current number of elements in queue
• QUEUE OVERFLOW: Trying to enqueue when queue is full
• QUEUE UNDERFLOW: Trying to dequeue when queue is empty

🔹 VISUAL REPRESENTATION:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Empty Queue:
    FRONT → [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

After enqueue(10):
    FRONT → [10] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

After enqueue(20):
    FRONT → [10] [20] [ ] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

After enqueue(30):
    FRONT → [10] [20] [30] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

After dequeue() - removes 10:
            FRONT → [20] [30] [ ] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

After enqueue(40):
            FRONT → [20] [30] [40] [ ] [ ] ← REAR

Operations Flow:
enqueue(10) → enqueue(20) → enqueue(30) → dequeue() returns 10 
→ enqueue(40) → front() returns 20

===============================================================================
           SECTION 3: SIMPLE QUEUE (LINEAR QUEUE) IMPLEMENTATION
===============================================================================

🔹 WHY ARRAY IMPLEMENTATION?
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Advantages:
✅ Simple and easy to implement
✅ Cache-friendly (contiguous memory)
✅ Fast access time O(1)
✅ No extra memory for pointers

Disadvantages:
❌ Fixed size (must be declared initially)
❌ Wastage of memory (cannot reuse dequeued space)
❌ Queue overflow even with empty spaces
❌ Cannot grow dynamically

🔹 IMPLEMENTATION APPROACH:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. Use an array to store elements
2. Use 'front' to track first element index
3. Use 'rear' to track last element index
4. Initialize front = -1, rear = -1 (indicates empty queue)
5. For enqueue: increment rear, then add element
6. For dequeue: increment front, then return element

⚠️ MAJOR PROBLEM WITH SIMPLE QUEUE:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Once front moves forward, the space before it is WASTED!

Example:
Initial: [10][20][30][ ][ ]  front=0, rear=2
After 2 dequeues: [ ][ ][30][ ][ ]  front=2, rear=2
Try enqueue(40): [ ][ ][30][40][ ]  front=2, rear=3
Try enqueue(50): [ ][ ][30][40][50]  front=2, rear=4 (FULL!)

But we have 2 empty spaces at the beginning! This is the problem.
Solution: Use CIRCULAR QUEUE (covered in next section)

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                    SIMPLE QUEUE - JAVA IMPLEMENTATION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
*/

class SimpleQueue {
    private int[] arr;        // Array to store queue elements
    private int front;        // Index of front element
    private int rear;         // Index of rear element
    private int capacity;     // Maximum capacity of queue
    private int size;         // Current number of elements
    
    // Constructor - Initialize queue with given capacity
    public SimpleQueue(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        arr = new int[capacity];
        front = -1;           // -1 indicates empty queue
        rear = -1;
        size = 0;
    }
    
    /**
     * ENQUEUE OPERATION - Add element to rear of queue
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public void enqueue(int element) {
        // Check for Queue Overflow
        if (isFull()) {
            System.out.println("Queue Overflow! Cannot enqueue " + element);
            return;
        }
        
        // If queue is empty, set front to 0
        if (front == -1) {
            front = 0;
        }
        
        // Increment rear and add element
        rear++;
        arr[rear] = element;
        size++;
        System.out.println(element + " enqueued to queue");
    }
    
    /**
     * DEQUEUE OPERATION - Remove and return front element
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int dequeue() {
        // Check for Queue Underflow
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue Underflow! Queue is empty");
            return -1;
        }
        
        int removed = arr[front];
        
        // If this was the last element, reset queue
        if (front == rear) {
            front = -1;
            rear = -1;
        } else {
            front++;
        }
        
        size--;
        return removed;
    }
    
    /**
     * FRONT/PEEK OPERATION - View front element without removing
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int front() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return -1;
        }
        return arr[front];
    }
    
    /**
     * REAR OPERATION - View rear element
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int rear() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return -1;
        }
        return arr[rear];
    }
    
    /**
     * CHECK IF QUEUE IS EMPTY
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return front == -1;
    }
    
    /**
     * CHECK IF QUEUE IS FULL
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public boolean isFull() {
        return rear == capacity - 1;
    }
    
    /**
     * GET CURRENT SIZE OF QUEUE
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
    
    /**
     * DISPLAY ALL ELEMENTS (for debugging)
     * Time Complexity: O(n)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public void display() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return;
        }
        
        System.out.print("Queue elements (front to rear): ");
        for (int i = front; i <= rear; i++) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

/*
===============================================================================
                SECTION 4: CIRCULAR QUEUE IMPLEMENTATION
===============================================================================

🔹 WHAT IS CIRCULAR QUEUE?
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

A Circular Queue is an improved version of Simple Queue where the LAST 
position is connected back to the FIRST position, forming a circle.

Key Advantage: Solves the memory wastage problem of simple queue!

Visual Representation:

    [0] [1] [2] [3] [4]
     ↑               ↓
     └───────────────┘
     (Connected in circle)

🔹 HOW IT WORKS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Instead of rear reaching capacity-1 and declaring "FULL", we check if the 
space is actually occupied or just unused due to dequeues.

Formula for circular increment:
    rear = (rear + 1) % capacity
    front = (front + 1) % capacity

Example:
Capacity = 5, Current state: [ ][ ][30][40][50]  front=2, rear=4

Enqueue(60): rear = (4+1) % 5 = 0
Result: [60][ ][30][40][50]  front=2, rear=0

Enqueue(70): rear = (0+1) % 5 = 1
Result: [60][70][30][40][50]  front=2, rear=1  (Now FULL!)

🔹 FULL CONDITION IN CIRCULAR QUEUE:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Queue is FULL when: (rear + 1) % capacity == front

This means the next position of rear would collide with front.

🔹 EMPTY CONDITION:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Method 1: Use size variable (easier)
    isEmpty() when size == 0

Method 2: Use front and rear only
    isEmpty() when front == -1

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                    CIRCULAR QUEUE - JAVA IMPLEMENTATION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
*/

class CircularQueue {
    private int[] arr;
    private int front;
    private int rear;
    private int capacity;
    private int size;
    
    /**
     * Constructor - Initialize circular queue
     */
    public CircularQueue(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        arr = new int[capacity];
        front = -1;
        rear = -1;
        size = 0;
    }
    
    /**
     * ENQUEUE OPERATION - Add element to queue (circular)
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public void enqueue(int element) {
        // Check for overflow
        if (isFull()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is Full! Cannot enqueue " + element);
            return;
        }
        
        // First element being added
        if (front == -1) {
            front = 0;
        }
        
        // Circular increment of rear
        rear = (rear + 1) % capacity;
        arr[rear] = element;
        size++;
        System.out.println(element + " enqueued to circular queue");
    }
    
    /**
     * DEQUEUE OPERATION - Remove element from front (circular)
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int dequeue() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is Empty! Cannot dequeue");
            return -1;
        }
        
        int removed = arr[front];
        
        // If this was the last element
        if (front == rear) {
            front = -1;
            rear = -1;
        } else {
            // Circular increment of front
            front = (front + 1) % capacity;
        }
        
        size--;
        return removed;
    }
    
    /**
     * FRONT/PEEK OPERATION
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int front() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return -1;
        }
        return arr[front];
    }
    
    /**
     * REAR OPERATION
     * Time Complexity: O(1)
     */
    public int rear() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return -1;
        }
        return arr[rear];
    }
    
    /**
     * CHECK IF EMPTY
     */
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
        // Alternative: return front == -1;
    }
    
    /**
     * CHECK IF FULL
     * Critical condition for circular queue!
     */
    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
        // Alternative: return (rear + 1) % capacity == front && front != -1;
    }
    
    /**
     * GET SIZE
     */
    public int size() {
        return size;
    }
    
    /**
     * DISPLAY - Shows elements in order (handles circular nature)
     * Time Complexity: O(n)
     */
    public void display() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty!");
            return;
        }
        
        System.out.print("Circular Queue (front to rear): ");
        int i = front;
        while (true) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
            if (i == rear) break;
            i = (i + 1) % capacity;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

