🔹 QUEUE OVERFLOW:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Occurs when: Trying to enqueue into full queue

In Simple Queue:
- Occurs when rear == capacity - 1
- Even if front has moved forward (memory waste)

In Circular Queue:
- Occurs when (rear + 1) % capacity == front
- Truly full, no wasted space

Solutions:
1. Check isFull() before enqueuing
2. Use circular queue instead of simple queue
3. Use dynamic size (linked list)
4. Resize array (ArrayList approach)

Prevention Code:
```java
public void enqueue(int x) {
    if (isFull()) {
        // Option 1: Throw exception
        throw new RuntimeException("Queue is full");
        
        // Option 2: Return false
        return false;
        
        // Option 3: Resize (for dynamic queue)
        resize();
    }
    // Proceed with enqueue
}
```

🔹 QUEUE UNDERFLOW:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Occurs when: Trying to dequeue from empty queue

Causes:
- More dequeue operations than enqueue
- Not checking isEmpty() before dequeue

Solutions:
1. Always check isEmpty() before dequeue
2. Handle gracefully with sentinel value or exception

Prevention Code:
```java
public int dequeue() {
    if (isEmpty()) {
        // Option 1: Return sentinel
        return Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        
        // Option 2: Throw exception
        throw new RuntimeException("Queue is empty");
    }
    // Proceed with dequeue
}
```

